// OVER-REFUSAL, tier 3 — legal Rust, refused. `loop { x = 7; break; }` assigns
// `x` on every path that leaves the loop, so after it `x` IS definitely
// initialised and the read (or borrow) is legal. logosc refuses with
//     error [fn main]: use of possibly uninitialised binding 'x'
// ROOT, read per-site: `lower_loop` (src/compiler/sema_stmt.cpp, the
// `loop_pre_uninit` snapshot/restore pair) says in its own comment "loops are
// CONSERVATIVE — the body may run zero times". That is TRUE of `while` and
// `for` and FALSE of `loop`, whose body always executes at least once; the
// same restore is used for all three, so every initialisation performed in a
// `loop` body is discarded at the join even when the only exit is a `break`
// below it.
// PRE-EXISTING, and this is the CONTROL that says so: the plain READ shown
// below is refused on the BASE binary (`c4594d5f9256736c`, HEAD f04eb6aae)
// with the identical sentence, i.e. by `lower_var_ref`'s own check at
// sema_expr.cpp:874, with no borrow anywhere in the program. The 2026-09-12k
// definite-assignment class fix asks that SAME predicate at the address-mint
// sites, so it inherits this over-refusal for `&x` — it does not create it and
// does not widen it: the set of programs the new sites refuse is a subset of
// what a read at the same point already refused.
// ⚠ THE LEGALITY CLAIM RESTS ON A READING OF RUST, NOT ON RUSTC: there is no
// rustc binary on this box. The upstream evidence that the reading is right is
// tests/ui/borrowck/borrowck-break-uninit.rs @ da5114692c9, which had to put
// `break;` BEFORE `x = 0;` to obtain E0381 — the assign-then-break order is
// exactly the one upstream does not diagnose.
// FIX: `loop` must not restore the pre-body uninit set wholesale; a `loop`
// whose body assigns before every reachable `break` leaves the binding
// initialised. Its own row, its own round — `while`/`for` keep the
// conservative restore.
package loop_init_before_break_refuses;
fn main() -> i32 {
    let mut x: i64;
    loop {
        x = 7i64;
        break;
    }
    return x as i32;
}
